deep.reconstruction:
Context (previous and next pages): X is a connected Riemann surface. For each point x in X, take the universal covering of X based at x and uniformize it as P^1, C or the disc inside P^1. As x varies, these uniformizations fit into a relative object over X:
- an open set X̃ inside a P^1-bundle P_X → X;
- a map p_X : X̃ → X×X, which is fibrewise the universal covering;
- a section σ of P_X (the image of the base point x);
- a flat connection c_X on P_X. The connection exists because two uniformizations differ by a Möbius transformation, so the fibres P^1_x are canonically identified by analytic continuation.
The previous page identifies X̃ near σ with the tangent bundle and treats the cases X = A^1 and X = G_m.

This page selects the parts of this canonical transcendental structure that keep an algebraic meaning when X is algebraic. In the proper case this follows from GAGA. The parts are:
(a) the P^1-bundle P_X over X;
(b) the flat connection c_X on P_X, relative to X/C;
(c) the section σ.
In square brackets he adds (d): an isomorphism of formal schemes over X, from the completion of P_X along σ to the completion of X×X along the diagonal Δ_X, compatible with the connections. This is the formal germ of the fibrewise developing map, i.e. the formal inverse of p_X.

He remarks that (d) is determined by (b) and (c) (the manuscript says 'b) et d)', clearly meaning b) and c)). This holds provided σ is nowhere stationary, i.e. everywhere transverse to the horizontal foliation of c_X. That condition is automatically satisfied when a compatible isomorphism (d) exists.

In modern terms, (a)+(b)+(c) with the transversality condition is exactly a PGL_2-oper, equivalently a complex projective structure on X. Here it is the uniformizing projective structure. (d) is the statement that an oper determines the formal developing map.

For non-proper X, Deligne's Riemann–Hilbert correspondence (1970) gives existence and uniqueness of an algebraic structure on (a), (b) inducing the transcendental one, under the condition that c_X is regular at infinity. The marginal addition says this also implies algebraicity of (c). Regularity does hold for the uniformization (punctures give parabolic, regular-singular local behaviour).

He then announces he will establish uniqueness and attack existence 'par une autre voie', presumably algebraically. The next page asks whether σ and p_X admit a purely algebraic description. This is essentially the accessory-parameter problem for the uniformizing oper.
